# 023 · Stop, start and terminate RunPod rigs

**Status:** `IN_PROGRESS` · **Version:** [v0.1.0](../roadmap.md#v010) · **Part:** Rigs on RunPod · **Depends on:** [022](022-runpod-launch.goal)

## What

A `runpod` lifecycle driver beside the compose and Kubernetes ones: stop, start, terminate, restart and full reset, mapped to RunPod's API. Each change is an event in the rig's history. Terminate waits until the results have left the pod.

## Why

A rig you pay for by the hour must stop when it has no work, and it must not lose results when it ends.

## Done when

- [ ] Each action works against a fake RunPod API in CI, and once on RunPod.
- [x] Terminate refuses while exports are still pending.

## Decisions

- 2026-10-20, lead (brief): stop pauses the runs and sends waiting results out first; terminate waits until every result has left the pod (`resultsLeft()`, goal 024) and otherwise refuses, leaving the pod running with its runs paused; a full reset sends every result out, wipes the rig's state and keeps the pod and its GPU; start says plainly when RunPod has no GPU free; the owner alone, over REST, MCP and the command line, as for the launch; the dashboard's buttons wait for the owner's mockup.
- 2026-10-20, builder: restart is a stop and a start through RunPod's API (the two actions the RunPod spike, goal 018, confirmed), with no export: the volume keeps everything. Both parts restart together, since a pod runs them in one container.
- 2026-10-20, builder: a stop waits for its results at most 15 minutes, then stops the pod all the same: a stopped pod keeps its volume, and a limit's stop must not run on for long. What did not leave is named in the rig's history.
- 2026-10-20, builder: a terminate waits as a full reset does: no judge job and no command waiting (1 hour), a full export (30 minutes), every result off the pod (1 hour). Past a step's limit, or when a result can never leave (an export that failed, a supervisor with nowhere to send), it is refused. There is no forced terminate: RunPod's console still removes a pod, and control then marks it gone.
- 2026-10-20, builder: a start resumes the runs the last stop paused. A start is refused while the pod has cost its spend limit.
- 2026-10-20, builder: a rig whose harness never reported holds no result, so a stop or a terminate acts on its pod at once (a pod that never enrolled, say). A stopped pod is terminated at once only when its last stop's export, and every result with it, had left the pod.
- 2026-10-20, builder: the spend limit and the idle stop stop the pod through the same stop, its results first; they never remove it.
- 2026-10-20, builder: the routes every rig has carry restart and the full reset (`POST /v1/rigs/<rig>/restart` and `/reset`); stop, start and terminate are new, under `/v1/rigs/<rig>/runpod/`. MCP's `runpod_rig` (owner only) and `thermite runpod stop|start|restart|terminate|reset|confirm-wipe|cancel-reset` cover all of them. The RunPod driver serves only rigs launched on RunPod and needs no setting; `THERMITE_LIFECYCLE` still names the driver for the others.
- 2026-10-20, owner: A RunPod rig's page and lifecycle: approved, with the stop after a timeout when no campaign is running (blocked, stopped or finished runs included), as for 022.
- 2026-10-20, builder: The page's **Restart** box says what the restart does: a stop and a start of the pod through RunPod, not a restart of the programs in it as the mockup said. The rig's **⋯** menu keeps only **Retire it** for a RunPod rig: its changes are under **The pod**. The page's restart route sends a RunPod rig to RunPod's driver, and its full reset is refused while the pod is stopped, as over REST.
- 2026-10-22, owner: "Live on disk, copy to volume". RunPod's volume refuses even root a change of owner, so the rig image keeps its live state on the pod's own disk, with the owners it has everywhere else, and root copies it to a snapshot on the volume, which it restores at a start on an empty disk. Compose and Kubernetes rigs are unchanged: no snapshot unless `THERMITE_STATE_SNAPSHOT_DIR` is set.
- 2026-10-22, builder: the snapshot's rules. Every 5 minutes (`THERMITE_STATE_SNAPSHOT_MINUTES`), after each new export in the outbox (looked for every 10 s), right after enrollment (the pod's keys), and at `SIGTERM` before the parts are told to stop, for at most 8 s (`THERMITE_STATE_SNAPSHOT_STOP_SECONDS`; a second signal ends it). The ledger is copied with SQLite's backup (`node:sqlite` `backup()`, every page in one step) by a process that runs as the controller's user, first, so an export the ledger records is in the outbox's copy too. Unchanged files are hard links to the last snapshot; a volume that refuses links gets full copies. Each snapshot is written into `snapshot.new`, its manifest last, then renamed into place. The snapshot runs in a process of its own at a low priority (but at a stop), and one that falls due while a timed run is at work waits for it, up to 5 minutes. The snapshot folder is root's, 0700, never chowned; the broker tries to open it as the controller's user, the supervisor's and `agent-1` at start, and takes and restores nothing when any of them gets in (superseded the same day by the seal, below: an open folder no longer stops the snapshot, and unchanged files are no longer hard links). A restore takes plain files and folders only, under the parts and names a snapshot holds, with each part's owner and the recorded mode (no set-id bit on a file), all parts or none.

- 2026-10-22, owner: "Seal it". RunPod's volume ignores file permissions, so a folder of mode 0700 keeps no user out, and the snapshot holds the ledger and the pod's tunnel keys. The snapshot is sealed with a key control mints for each pod at its launch, which only root holds in the pod.
- 2026-10-22, builder: the seal. Control mints 32 random bytes for each launch (`THERMITE_STATE_SNAPSHOT_KEY`, base64url, a launch value) and keeps no copy: RunPod keeps it in the pod's settings, which a stopped pod keeps. The broker reads it first thing at start and deletes it from its own environment, and the controller's and supervisor's environments filter it out as well; the snapshot helper gets it on its standard input, never in its arguments. Without it no snapshot is taken or restored, and the log says why. HKDF-SHA-256 derives three keys from it: file contents, file names, manifest. Each file is AES-256-GCM with a fresh 12-byte nonce (`THMSEAL1`, a kind byte, the nonce, ciphertext, tag), and its name is the HMAC-SHA-256 of its content. The manifest (format 2: rig, sequence number, time, reason, and each entry's part, path, type, mode, owner, time, size, sealed name and SHA-256) is sealed whole. The folder is flat: `manifest`, `manifest.new` while one is written, the sealed files, `.new-<random>` while one is written. A file unchanged since the last snapshot whose sealed file is there is not read; content sealed already is not sealed again; after the rename onto `manifest`, sealed files no manifest names are removed. Root works from inside the folder (its working folder, checked to be the folder it entered) by bare names, so a user who swaps the folder for a link can't send root's writes or removals elsewhere. A restore takes the newest manifest that verifies and is this rig's, and every file must verify against it (size, tag, SHA-256, keyed hash); anything else refuses the whole snapshot, logs `the snapshot did not verify: <why>`, and the rig starts empty. An unsealed snapshot from the image before is never restored, and is removed only where no other user may write in the folder.
- 2026-10-22, builder: the isolation check. The snapshot folder passes as `sealed, closed to …` or, on a volume that lets the users in, `open, sealed: …`, as long as it holds only the sealed manifest, sealed files, files being sealed and empty probe files; a file without the seal's header, an unsealed snapshot or any other name fails (`FOUND …`). A new container check and a new role probe, `snapshot-key`, look for the key in every environment they can read. The roles' `state-snapshot-list` and `state-snapshot-create` probes are one probe, `state-snapshot`, which removes its empty file again.

## Open questions

- **An older sealed snapshot put back.** Sealing stops reading and changing the snapshot, not deleting it or putting back an older sealed snapshot of the same pod that an agent kept a copy of. Each manifest has a sequence number, but a restore runs on an empty disk, before the pod has talked to control, so it has nothing to compare it with: an older snapshot restores as if it were the newest. Detecting it needs control to keep the last number the rig reported, and the pod to fetch it before it restores (or to restore, then check before the harness starts). Not built: is the cost worth it, given that what left the pod is on control and an agent must survive a stop and a start for it to matter?

## Progress

- 2026-10-19: Goal written from the v0.1.0 release plan.
- 2026-10-20: Built behind the screen:
  - **The driver.** `RunpodDriver` (`src/control/lifecycle.ts`) beside the compose and Kubernetes drivers, and the changes themselves in `src/control/runpod-lifecycle.ts`, which `RunpodRigs` (`runpod-rigs.ts`) starts on the owner's ask or at a limit and moves on in control's loop, one change per rig at a time. A rig's `runpod` view has `op`, `op_words`, `paused_runs` and `last_export`; `stopped_by` is also `owner`.
  - **Stop:** pause the running runs, `export_now`, wait until it and every export and champion's patch waiting on the pod have reached control (or 15 minutes), then RunPod's stop. **Start:** RunPod's start; refused in words when RunPod has no GPU free (`RunPod has no free GPU for pod … now, so the pod stays stopped (…)`), and while the spend limit is reached; once the pod runs, the paused runs resume, and a pod with fewer GPUs than launched is said in the history. **Restart:** stop, then start once RunPod says it stopped. **Terminate:** pause, quiesce, full export, every result off the pod, then RunPod's delete; the rig is retired. Refused otherwise, with the pod running and its runs paused. **Full reset** (`lifecycle.ts`): only while the pod runs and no change is under way; its `verifying` step also waits for `resultsLeft()`; at the wipe the pod and its GPU stay.
  - **History:** each change is a step of the rig's history (`runpod_launched`, `runpod_stopping`, `runpod_stopped`, `runpod_restarting`, `runpod_starting`, `runpod_start_refused`, `runpod_running`, `runpod_terminating`, `runpod_terminated`, `runpod_terminate_refused`, `runpod_gone`, `reset_requested`, `reset_done`, `reset_failed`, `reset_cancelled`), in `GET /v1/rigs/<rig>/runpod` (`history`) and MCP's `rig_setup`, and in the audit log.
  - **Who:** the owner alone: `POST /v1/rigs/<rig>/runpod/stop|start|terminate`, `POST /v1/rigs/<rig>/restart|reset` (RunPod's driver serves RunPod rigs with no setting), MCP's `runpod_rig`, and `thermite runpod stop|start|restart|terminate|reset|confirm-wipe|cancel-reset`.
  - **Spend limit:** reaching it, or the idle stop, now goes through the stop (its results first); it never removes the pod.
  - Tests: `src/control/runpod-lifecycle.test.ts` (every action and refusal against the fake RunPod API, terminate refusing while exports or a champion's patch wait, while a result can never leave, and after a failed export; the full reset keeping the pod; REST and MCP refusing every token but the owner's), `src/control/runpod-rigs.test.ts` (the idle stop's export first), `src/cli/runpod.test.ts`. `scripts/runpod-acceptance.mjs` runs launch, enrollment, a report, stop, start and terminate against the fake RunPod API with pods that are containers of the rig image; it passed under rootless Podman, and CI's new `runpod` job runs it with Docker.
  - Pages: Run a rig on RunPod (new), Stop, restart, remove or reset a rig, REST API (Stop, start, restart and terminate; restart and reset; audit actions), MCP tools (`runpod_rig`, `rig_setup`), Command line, Tokens and roles, Exports, Known limits, Trust between control and rigs.
  - Left: the dashboard's buttons and the history on the RunPod rig's page (they wait for the owner's mockup; each step's words are in `RUNPOD_STEP_WORDS`, which the REST history already gives), the first CI run of the `runpod` job, and the actions once on RunPod, in the paid acceptance (goal 025), which needs the owner's OK to spend. **Restart the rig's containers** in the rig page's **⋯** menu still goes to `THERMITE_LIFECYCLE`'s driver only.
- 2026-10-20: Every action passes against the fake RunPod API in CI (CI run 39479049337 on `v1/build` (85b4de0), the `runpod` job). Left for the box: once on RunPod, in the paid run.
- 2026-10-20: The dashboard, from the approved mockup (`v010-runpod-rig.html`): **The pod** on the rig's page with **Stop the pod**, **Start the pod**, **Restart the pod**, **Start the full reset** and **Terminate** (typed words for the last two), each off with the reason when it can't apply; the change under way with its steps; **Results in the pod** from the supervisor's last report and the last export; **History** with the pod's steps beside the rig's events and filters for the pod, the runs and the judge; a terminated rig's page with what it cost. A stopped pod shows as stopped, not stale or offline. Routes: `POST /ui/rigs/<rig>/runpod/stop|start|restart|terminate|limits`. Tests: `src/control/runpod-ui.test.ts`. Pages: Run a rig on RunPod, A rig's page, Stop, restart, remove or reset a rig. Left: the actions once on RunPod, in the paid run (goal 025).
- 2026-10-21: Fixes from the docs review. A refused terminate (`Not terminated: …`) stays on the rig's page, in REST's `error` and `refusal`, and in `thermite runpod list` until the owner's next stop, start, restart or terminate, or a newer step in the rig's history; a good read of the pod no longer clears it (`0e7c31c`). The Restart box and its steps say what a restart does: no run is paused, and a trial in progress stops and is planned again (`071b969`). Tests: `src/control/runpod-ui.test.ts`.

- 2026-10-22: **Finding** (a real pod's container log): RunPod's persistent volume at `/workspace` refuses `chown` even for root (`EPERM`). The rig image kept the state of both parts there, so the broker could not give the harness's state, the outbox or the supervisor's folder their owners, and an uncaught `chown -R` of the supervisor's folder stopped it at every start: RunPod restarted the container every 16 s and nothing answered on the pull mode port, so the pod never enrolled. The test pods had never mounted a volume, and the fake RunPod's volume (a Podman named volume) allows `chown`.
  **Fix:** the rig image's live state moves to `/var/lib/thermite-rig` on the pod's own disk, and `THERMITE_STATE_SNAPSHOT_DIR=/workspace/thermite` holds a snapshot of it (`src/harness/broker/snapshot.ts`, `snapshot-main.ts`, `broker.ts`, `containers/rig/Containerfile`; the rules are under Decisions). The isolation selftest probes the snapshot folder for every role (`state-snapshot-list`, `state-snapshot-create`) and checks the broker's record of it (`state-snapshot`). The launch form's **Storage** help says the volume keeps a snapshot.
  Tests: `src/harness/broker/snapshot.test.ts` (a take and a restore onto an empty disk with owners and modes, and every `chown` on the live side; the ledger copied while another process writes it, each copy whole; the swap by rename, a failed take and a take stopped half way leaving the snapshot before it; links, pipes and another user's files never copied, and links, pipes and names that climb out refused at a restore; all-or-nothing restore, never over live state; a volume that refuses hard links; the rig image's layout, with its live folders off the pod's volume and its keys in the snapshot; compose and cluster rigs setting nothing; the schedule: one at a time, retried after a failure, waiting for timed runs, the stop's last one and its time limit; the helper process), `src/harness/selftest.test.ts`. The rig-image acceptance checks the snapshot folder (root's, 0700, closed to both parts and the roles), the snapshot after enrollment and at the stop, and the new probes. The RunPod acceptance checks the stop's snapshot, that nothing on the pod's volume belongs to anyone but root, and the start's restore with the same token; the pod reports again with its keys and enrollment. A volume that refuses `chown` could not be made under rootless Podman (an idmapped mount is refused there), so the acceptance checks the volume's owners instead. Both acceptances passed under rootless Podman (the stop's snapshot took under 0.1 s); not run with Docker here.
  Pages: the harness image reference (Where the state lives), Settings, Run a rig on RunPod (what a stop keeps), Stop, restart, remove or reset a rig, Troubleshooting (the `chown` case, and a rig that lost its state over a stop), Rig checks, Isolation, Ports, volumes and secrets, Command line, REST API, MCP tools. Left: once on RunPod, in the paid run (goal 025): the owner and mode the real volume shows for root's files, whether it takes hard links, and how long a stop gives the container.
- 2026-10-22: **Finding** (a real pod's isolation check at start): RunPod's volume ignores file permissions. The snapshot folder, root's with mode 0700, was open to the controller's user, the supervisor's and `agent-1`, each of which listed it and wrote in it, and every role's `state-snapshot-list` and `state-snapshot-create` probes were `ALLOWED`. The check failed, as it should, and the broker took no snapshot, so a stop would have lost the state; the snapshot holds the ledger and the pod's tunnel keys. Mode bits can't protect it there, and the volume refuses `chown` too. The owner chose to seal it (Decisions).
  **Built:** `src/harness/broker/seal.ts` (the key, HKDF, AES-256-GCM for whole buffers and for files as they stream, names by HMAC), `snapshot.ts` (the flat sealed folder, incremental by size and time and by content, the swap of `manifest.new` onto `manifest` and the sweep after it, the restore that verifies every file and refuses the whole snapshot otherwise, root working from inside the folder, the unsealed snapshot of the image before, `inspectSnapshotFolder`), `snapshot-main.ts` (the job and key on standard input), `broker.ts` (the key taken out of the environment first; no key, no snapshot; a refused snapshot logged and the rig started empty), `one-container.ts` (the key filtered out of both parts' environments), `selftest.ts` (`state-snapshot` passes `open, sealed`, fails on any unsealed file; `snapshot-key` as a container check and a role probe), `src/control/tunnel.ts` and `src/core/tunnel.ts` (a key minted for each launch, never kept), `src/common/env.ts` (`redact` takes out the value of a variable named like a key, code, token, secret or prefix), `runpod.ts` and `runpod-rigs.ts` (a RunPod refusal is redacted before it is cut short, and the launch's secret values are scrubbed from it).
  Tests: `src/harness/broker/seal.test.ts` (the key's form and its removal from the environment; distinct keys per purpose; round trips; another key, another kind, any changed byte refused), `snapshot.test.ts` (the take and restore with owners and modes, no plaintext of the state on the volume; the ledger consistent while written; the swap and a take cut off; incremental reuse, the same content sealed once, the sweep, a removed sealed file sealed again; a changed sealed file, a changed manifest, two sealed files swapped by name, one cut short, one missing, one a link, another pod's snapshot and another rig's each refused whole with nothing restored; a verifying manifest's bad names refused one by one; the unsealed snapshot never restored, removed only when the folder is closed; a folder swapped for a link refused; what the folder may hold; the helper with its key on standard input, the key never in its output), `src/harness/selftest.test.ts` (each outcome of `state-snapshot`; `snapshot-key` in root's processes only; the roles' probes, and their look agreeing with the check's), `one-container.test.ts` (no part's environment has the key), `src/control/tunnel.test.ts` and `runpod-rigs.test.ts` (a new key per launch, in RunPod's settings only: not in control's database, audit, views or log; a refusal that echoes it told without it), `src/common/env.test.ts` (redaction).
  Acceptance, under rootless Podman: the rig-image acceptance (33 checks) now also checks that the snapshot after enrollment is sealed (the manifest and sealed files only, nothing in plain text), that neither part's environment and none of the rig's output has the key, the container checks `state-snapshot` (`sealed, closed to …; holds the manifest and 14 sealed files`) and `snapshot-key`, and every role's `state-snapshot` (`EACCES`) and `snapshot-key` (`none`) probes; the stop's sealed snapshot took 0.22 s for 48 files. The RunPod acceptance (24 checks, against the fake RunPod API) checks the launch's key, the sealed volume after the stop, and the start's restore from the sealed manifest, every file verified, with the same keys, enrollment and token. Both passed; not run with Docker here. A volume that ignores file modes could not be made here, so `open, sealed` is checked by the unit tests only.
  Pages: the harness image reference (Where the state lives: the seal, what it does not stop; How it starts; Paths), Settings (`THERMITE_STATE_SNAPSHOT_KEY`), Rig checks (`state-snapshot`, `snapshot-key`), Isolation, Trust between control and rigs (the one launch secret that lasts as long as the pod), Who holds your keys, Run a rig on RunPod (what a stop keeps), Troubleshooting (no key; the snapshot did not verify, and why the pod can't enroll again), Ports, volumes and secrets, REST API, CHANGELOG. Left for the paid run (goal 025): the isolation check's `open, sealed` on a real pod, and whether RunPod's volume takes the renames, removals and `fsync` the seal uses.

